<a class="skip" href="#work">Skip to the builds</a>

{/* The wood: one fixed layer; everything scrolls over it. */}
<div class="ground" aria-hidden="true"></div>

<div class="one" data-one>
  {/* ── HERO — the sitemap ─────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="hero" aria-label="Jonathan Tollefson — site index">
    {/* the corner is gone (Jon, 2026-10-13 round six: "it breaks the laws of
        the design system to have the résumé button floating"): the rail's
        foot carries résumé as its one button, the bar its pill, and the page
        ends on it (#end) */}
    <header class="hero-name">
      <div class="etch-slot">
        {etchAsset ? (
          <>
            <h1 class="sr-only">Jonathan Tollefson</h1>
            <img class="etch-img" src={etchAsset} alt="" draggable="false" />
          </>
        ) : (
          <h1 class="etch-text">Jonathan Tollefson</h1>
        )}
        <p class="role-line">design engineer · audio &amp; real-time web</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    {/* the cue (2026-10-13): one word at the hero's foot, so the page says
        it goes on. No motion, no peek (Jon: "the down arrow with builds is
        enough by itself"). */}
    <a class="cue" href="#work"><span class="cue-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</span> builds <span class="cue-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
  </section>

  {/* ── THE LINE (2026-10-22, night; Jon: "too much text on the landing screen. 'My job doesn't pay me…' should be
      in its own view beneath the arrows, almost like how résumé on the bottom has its own real estate, so that when
      they see it, it's all they're seeing"): one full-viewport room between the hero and the first sheet, his
      sentence ALONE, centered, in the end room's title face. Not a rail target (no NAV row, no BUILD_ORDER entry): the
      comet crosses it as ground between the hero's knot and intake's. His words, not DRAFT */}
  <section class="room line" id="line" aria-label="Why I build these">
    <p class="line-say">My job doesn't pay me to build these. But I use them every day.</p>
  </section>

  {/* ── THE SIDEBAR — persistent left column, nav only (Jon, 2026-08-16:
      always present from load; flat light panel; mobile deferred) ───────── */}
  <nav class="rail" data-rail aria-label="Sections">
    {/* the JT mark at the rail's head (Jon, 2026-10-15: "JT logo needs to
        be on the landing page as well") — the favicon's letterforms in the
        rail's ink, the gradient on hover, the same object the away rail
        wears; absolute so the centred column and the comet's knots never
        move for it */}
    <a class="rail-jt" href="/" aria-label="Jonathan Tollefson — the studio">
      <svg viewBox="3.5 6.5 76.5 29" aria-hidden="true">
        <defs>
          <linearGradient id="railjt-grad" x1="3.5" y1="21" x2="80" y2="21" gradientUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
            <stop offset="0" stop-color="#BC56B0" />
            <stop offset="0.47" stop-color="#F71A8F" />
            <stop offset="0.77" stop-color="#F77C59" />
            <stop offset="1" stop-color="#FC9755" />
          </linearGradient>
        </defs>
        <path d="M41.692 7.30743L41.692 20.058C41.692 29.3223 34.655 35.0036 23.1957 35.0036C14.2219 35.0036 7.02344 31.13 4.27965 24.7708L10.316 22.9632C12.2851 27.1273 17.1916 29.7097 23.1957 29.7097C30.8137 29.7097 35.4943 26.0298 35.4943 20.058L35.4943 7.30743L41.692 7.30743Z" />
        <path d="M79.0212 12.6336L60.3635 12.6336L60.3635 34.0352L54.1658 34.0352L54.1658 12.6336L35.508 12.6336L35.508 7.30743L79.0212 7.30743L79.0212 12.6336Z" />
      </svg>
    </a>
    {/* words alone (Jon, 2026-10-13, round two): the builds, then the art
        rooms. One face, one row height, every word ending on one x (so each
        knot on the comet's track sits the same distance from its word). No
        group words, no numerals, no glyphs, and since round seven no line
        between the blocks (Jon: "or just have them figure it out
        themselves"): six rows, five equal gaps. */}
    <div class="rail-col">
      {NAV.map((g) => (
        <>
          <ul class="rail-list" role="list" data-block={g.key}>
            {g.entries.map((e) => (
              <li data-room={e.room}>
                <a class="rail-item" href={`#${e.id}`} data-for={e.id}>
                  <span class="rail-word">{e.word}</span>
                </a>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </>
      ))}
    </div>
    <span class="comet" data-comet aria-hidden="true">
      <i class="comet-track"></i>
      <i class="comet-fill"></i>
      <i class="comet-bead"></i>
    </span>
    {/* résumé, the rail's one button (Jon, 2026-10-13 round six: "in the nav
        bar, on the bottom, and have it look like a button, visually a bit
        different than the other words"): an outlined pill above the switch.
        Round seven: it is also the comet's LAST knot (data-for="end"): while
        the end room spans the screen's centre line the pill is lit and the
        bead sits on it (Jon: "the nav/scroll should go down to résumé") */}
    <a class="rail-cv" href="/resume" data-for="end">résumé<span class="rail-cv-glyph" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    <button class="rail-night" type="button" data-night-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="Night mode" data-rail-night>
      <svg class="rn-glyph rn-sun" viewBox="0 0 12 12" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
        stroke-width="1.3" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true">
        <circle cx="6" cy="6" r="2.6" />
        <path d="M6 0.6v1.6M6 9.8v1.6M0.6 6h1.6M9.8 6h1.6" />
      </svg>
      <span class="rn-slot" aria-hidden="true"><i class="rn-thumb"></i></span>
      <svg class="rn-glyph rn-moon" viewBox="0 0 12 12" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
        stroke-width="1.3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
        <path d="M9.8 7.6A4.4 4.4 0 0 1 4.4 2.2a4.4 4.4 0 1 0 5.4 5.4Z" />
      </svg>
    </button>
  </nav>

  {/* ── the MOBILE bar, the rail's phone form (2026-08-24): the words at
      the thumb, night switch at the end. Same spy lights both: "builds" (the
      work room) lights while any build's sheet is the current one (it holds
      the work room's two by containment, and names the others in data-holds). */}
  <nav class="mbar" data-mbar aria-label="Sections">
    {BAR.map((m) => (
      <a
        class="mbar-item"
        href={`#${m.id}`}
        data-for={m.id}
        data-holds={m.holds}
      >
        <span class="mbar-word">{m.word}</span>
      </a>
    ))}
    <a class="mbar-item mbar-resume" href="/resume" data-for="end"><span class="mbar-word mbar-pill">résumé<span class="mbar-cv-glyph" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></span></a>
    <button class="mbar-night" type="button" data-night-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="Night mode">
      <svg class="mn-moon" viewBox="0 0 12 12" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
        stroke-width="1.3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
        <path d="M9.8 7.6A4.4 4.4 0 0 1 4.4 2.2a4.4 4.4 0 1 0 5.4 5.4Z" />
      </svg>
      <svg class="mn-sun" viewBox="0 0 12 12" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
        stroke-width="1.3" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true">
        <circle cx="6" cy="6" r="2.6" />
        <path d="M6 0.6v1.6M6 9.8v1.6M0.6 6h1.6M9.8 6h1.6" />
      </svg>
    </button>
  </nav>

  {/* ── SECTIONS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  {/* ── WORK — the story told through OBJECTS (2026-09-18, Jon: the matrix
      is scrapped). Object one: the Intake hero (Jon's Claude Design module,
      IntakeHero.astro + src/data/intake-run.json + public/intake/
      watch-blank.png): the watch mid-recording at the room's left edge;
      tap stop and the real run plays to its right (saved → transcript →
      verdict → ticket → the deck). The story words stay locked in
      builds-copy.ts; where they print under the run is the next round. */}
  {SHOW.has("work") && <section class="work room" id="work" aria-labelledby="work-t">
    <h2 id="work-t" class="sr-only">Work</h2>
    <div class="objects">
      {/* THE SHEETS (2026-10-10, Jon: "the titles go above the items; each
          module wrapped in its whole sheet that can expand", for a first-time
          visitor who knows nothing of him). Each build is ONE sheet: the band
          (its name + what it is) above the object, the object and its run,
          then the fold — the hook opens the story, the story and under the
          hood unfold inside the same sheet. AudioDex first: it answers the
          "audio" in the role line. */}
      {/* INTAKE FIRST (Jon, 2026-10-15: "a better hook, more entertaining"; Siri is the laugh): the watch (Jon's Claude Design module, 2026-09-18) */}
      <article class="wk sheet" id="build-watch" aria-labelledby="build-watch-t">
        <header class="band">
          <p class="sheet-kind">{WATCH_COPY.kind}</p>
          <h3 class="band-name" id="build-watch-t">{WATCH_COPY.title}</h3>
          <p class="band-what">{WATCH_COPY.dek}</p>
        </header>
        {/* THE CATCH-ALL (2026-10-21, branch intake-v4, fresh eyes after the pile; Jon: "the view of the calendar
            isn't good enough to be relevant … I don't know why we need the phone to be on the right side as well …
            too many other notifications with extra words … tap what's yours is stupid and weird"): the lock screen as
            it was, FIVE things he sent himself and nothing else, each its own press; beside it INTAKE'S CARDS in a
            hairline outline of the phone (round two, 2026-10-21: no app chrome); beside those HIS CALENDAR, a real month that pages to the day a
            landing takes. A press opens the thing, flies it into the Stream as its card, and the dated ones land on
            the month; the fifth is a memo from the wrist that plays, its words typed as spoken; no hint, ever
            (IntakeCatchAll.astro + src/data/intake-catchall.json). The watch under it is the live memo */}
        <IntakeCatchAll />
        {/* THE GAP (2026-10-19, Jon: "the spacing needs to be better, watch is too close"): the watch is its own
            section of the sheet, a .sheet-sec hairline between the send's foot and it, no label */}
        {/* THE CUT (round five, 2026-10-22, Jon: "the watch has redundant things … it should feel more lightweight,
            like 'try it yourself'"): no caption under the watch; its one line at rest is the hint `try it` */}
